deep.reconstruction:
Context. On the previous page Grothendieck builds a functor P that sends each connected, simply connected non-singular analytic curve Y to a complex projective line P(Y). It comes with a functorial embedding i_Y : Y → P(Y)(C) that makes Y a homogeneous domain. By uniformization Y is P^1, C or the disc, sitting in P^1 as itself, as P^1 minus a point, or as a disc. He then globalizes this over an arbitrary non-singular curve X. For each x in X, take the universal cover X̃(x) based at x. As x moves, these covers form a local system on the fundamental groupoid Π(X), and the projections X̃(x) → X give a horizontal morphism X̃ → X × X over X (via pr_2). The tautological base points give a section σ, which maps to the diagonal.

This page. Applying the functor P fibrewise turns that local system into a canonical P^1-bundle P_X over X. It carries an integrable (flat) connection and a horizontal open embedding X̃ ⊂ P_X. Since X̃ has a canonical analytic section σ, so does P_X.

Theorem 2. For a (countable at infinity) analytic curve X there exist, unique up to unique isomorphism:
- (a) an analytic P^1-bundle P_X over X;
- (b) an integrable connection on P_X relative to X/C;
- (c) a section σ of P_X;
- (d) an open subset X̃ of P_X containing σ(X);
- (e) a morphism of X-spaces p : X̃ → X_X = X × X.
These satisfy:
- (1) p∘σ = Δ_X;
- (2) for every x, p_x : X̃_x → X is a universal cover of the connected component of x;
- (3) p is horizontal;
- (4) each X̃_x is a homogeneous domain in the fibre (P_X)_x.

In modern terms this is the canonical (Fuchsian/uniformizing) projective structure on X, packaged as a flat P^1-bundle with a section that is nowhere horizontal: a PGL_2-oper in the Beilinson–Drinfeld sense, the uniformizing oper. The developing map is read off fibrewise, and the monodromy of the connection is the uniformizing representation π1(X) → PGL_2(C).

Why it matters for the dossier. This flat bundle is what underlies the uniformizing second-order linear ODE: the ratio of two solutions inverts the uniformization (Schwarzian, Poincaré–Klein–Fuchs). For modular and Shimura curves that equation is a Picard–Fuchs equation, which is presumably why it appears in a dossier on Gauss–Manin and Picard–Fuchs.

Next page. The three cases (P^1, C, disc) are worked out. In the parabolic case a second, horizontal section σ' appears, so P_X is the projective closure of a vector bundle.
